            <fr:display-uri>JO4V</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/JO4V/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Gather before, or reduce after">Gather before, or reduce after</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1786546736036</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p><fr:link href="/XGI0/" title="Sharding" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/XGI0/" display-uri="XGI0" type="local">Sharded</fr:link> matmul, case 2: one multiplicand has a sharded contracting dimension, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A[I, J_X] \cdot  B[J, K] \to  C[I, K]]]></fr:tex>. Which primitive, and what is the alternative?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:strong><fr:link href="/VSDZ/" title="AllGather" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/VSDZ/" display-uri="VSDZ" type="local">AllGather</fr:link> the sharded input first, then matmul locally</html:strong>: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {AllGather}_X(A[I, J_X]) \to  A[I, J]]]></fr:tex>, then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A[I, J] \cdot  B[J, K] \to  C[I, K]]]></fr:tex>. You cannot just multiply the local shards, because the sum over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[J]]></fr:tex> is split across <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> and each device holds only a partial sum.

  <html:strong>The alternative</html:strong> is to treat it like <fr:link href="/KIRV/" title="Matching contracting shards give partial sums" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/KIRV/" display-uri="KIRV" type="local">case 3</fr:link>: do the local partial matmul and <fr:link href="/U351/" title="AllReduce" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/U351/" display-uri="U351" type="local">AllReduce</fr:link> or <fr:link href="/CAGW/" title="ReduceScatter" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/CAGW/" display-uri="CAGW" type="local">ReduceScatter</fr:link> the result. That shards the FLOPs but usually costs more comms. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X[B,D] \cdot  Y[D_X,F]]]></fr:tex>, the gather moves <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2DF]]></fr:tex> bytes and the reduce moves <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2 \cdot  2BF]]></fr:tex>, so the reduce path wins roughly when <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D > 2B]]></fr:tex>.

  Rare in practice, though — the other input is usually sharded along the same axis anyway, as under FSDP.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
